# Build the structured LLM editor

## Scope and existing evidence

Historical notes below describe observed implementation gaps, which the
finite theoretical contract does not silently resolve.

Edit task instructions and notebook source with structured completion,
formatting,
object navigation, diagnostics, and a safe raw-response inspection boundary.

Current frontend evidence includes task, source, permanent prompt, backend-owned
options, and a visually isolated raw model-response boundary that explicitly
remains untrusted until backend validation. After a compatible authenticated
handshake, task, source, and raw-response draft fields are read from
backend-owned session state, staged without partial DOM updates, then committed
to the UI together before editing is enabled. Complete edits replace those same
backend draft fields, while page exit aborts both reads and writes and clears
browser text.

Structured completion, object navigation, candidate diagnostics, semantic
acceptance, and semantic-command transport remain unfinished.

## Finite structured source and untrusted-response contract

**Inputs:** one authenticated active session, backend-owned task/source drafts,
a current accepted notebook revision, a read-only semantic object index and
diagnostic set, and optional pasted model-response text. **Outputs:** editable
task/source drafts, visible raw response, bounded object navigation and linked
diagnostics. Draft edits alone never promote or replace accepted notebook data.

**Draft boundary:** a compatible session handshake and complete draft read must
precede enabling fields. Edits submit exact text as backend drafts, not as
commands to a browser-owned notebook. A failed or partial read keeps editing
disabled and identifies the failure without exposing stale session text.
Raw response stays text-only even when it contains markup, JSON-looking
commands, code, or export/start instructions; no paste executes or applies it.

**Finite structured aid:** for one notebook with one page, one title span and
one paragraph span, expose a tree of stable semantic identities and a source
cursor anchor. A completion suggestion may propose one admitted block kind at
an allowed insertion anchor; its preview is inert until a typed semantic edit
is validated and explicitly accepted. Formatting aids may insert literal
source characters only, not fabricate a parsed accepted notebook. Diagnostics
show severity, stable code, revision and target identity when those fields
are available; unknown targets remain a visible unresolved entry.

**Worked example:** accepted revision `r7` contains page `p1`, title `t1`
(`Study notes`) and paragraph `q1` (`La célula`). The user edits the Task
draft to `Outline biology`, highlights `q1` in the object tree, and pastes
`<script>start()</script>` in the raw-response field. The response is shown
verbatim as untrusted text, without script execution, semantic mutation,
Export, or a change from `r7`. A proposed paragraph insertion is merely a
suggestion until a separately accepted backend command succeeds.

**Refusal example:** if the object index is from `r6`, the current session
is `r7`, or a pasted response references a nonexistent `q9`, the editor
shows a stale/unresolved navigation result and retains the draft. It must
not guess the new identity or silently treat an unvalidated response as
accepted content. Session loss disables editing and requires a fresh read.

**Assumptions and limits:** authenticated backend draft synchronization and
inert raw text fields already exist. Object indexing, structured completion,
semantic diff/acceptance, and diagnostic-to-source transport are not fully
implemented. This finite design does not establish a working semantic code
editor, parser, or secure end-to-end response admission.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** practical for existing authenticated drafts and untrusted text
  presentation; theoretical for structured aids and semantic navigation,
  2026-11-02.
- **Delivered:** bounded notebook example, exact draft/response boundaries,
  optional inert completion, diagnostic linking and stale-session refusal.
- **Not implemented by this acceptance:** source-aware completion, object
  explorer, revision-linked diagnostics, validated candidate integration.
